#: processes/document-ingestion.bpmn:68
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "ONE OF TWO ENTRY POINTS, and this said \"the only\" one until 2026-09-20. A drop during an editing session triggers ingestion in the background; the contributor does not wait for it. THE OTHER is materialization: an asset listed in a remote graph that this instance, or one of its dependencies, declares in `remoteGraphs`. Owner, 2026-09-20: \"things can enter library through uploads/ -> library/ document ingestion or through retrieval / materialization of asset known through listed external KG in one of the dependent harnesses.\" That path runs materialize-remote.bpmn FIRST -- purpose, then the five gates -- and re-enters this process at \"Extract structure\" with a file that already carries its provenance and its fixity. It does NOT pass through uploads/, and that is deliberate: uploads/ is a queue for things somebody dropped, and a materialized asset was never dropped. Recording it as an upload would lose the one fact that distinguishes the two, which is that somebody decided it could be held."
msgstr ""

#: processes/document-ingestion.bpmn:165
#. Diagram label
msgid "Record the gap as a bean"
msgstr ""

#: processes/document-ingestion.bpmn:46
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Runs the whole pipeline from Task_Detect through Task_Promote — including the theme branch, which stays in THIS lane rather than dipping into Lane_2: deciding a document is a theme source is the engine asking its author a question, not a write to the shared work plan. It does not stop at a gap: Gateway_Complete's 'gap' branch leaves this lane only long enough for Lane_2 to record the bean, then Flow_i8 returns control here at CallActivity_Derive rather than ending the run — so an incomplete L1 build is this lane's problem to keep working until Gateway_Complete says otherwise."
msgstr ""

